Since $x \mapsto  rac{1+x}{x}$ is a decreasing function for $x > 0$, it is sufficient to prove the statement for $d = 2$. Recall the definition of $f_ho$ (for $ho <  rac{1}{3}$): $$f_ho(t) = \min_{\zeta \in [ho t,ho]} G(\zeta) + \zeta f_\Rade\left( rac{ho t}{\zeta}ight)$$ where $$G(\zeta) = -H(\{\zeta,p-\zeta,p-\zeta,1-2ho+\zeta\}) + 2H(ho).$$ We can bound the Rademacher rate function $f_\Rade$ by using the bound $H(a) \le \log 2 - 2(a- rac{1}{2})^2$ as follows: $$f_\Rade(t) \defeq \log 2 - H\left( rac{1+t}{2}ight) \ge  rac{t^2}{2}.$$ 
oindent Our lower bound shows non-detection provided that $$\lambda^2 < 2 \inf_{t \in (0,1)}\, \min_{\zeta \in [ho t,ho]}  rac{1+t^2}{t^2}\left(G(\zeta) +  rac{ho^2 t^2}{2 \zeta}ight) = 2 \inf_{\zeta \in (0,ho]} \, \inf_{t \in (0,\zeta/ho]} (1+t^2)\left( rac{G(\zeta)}{t^2} +  rac{ho^2}{2\zeta}ight),$$ or equivalently,  egin{equation} \lambda^2 < 2 \inf_{t \in (0,\zeta/ho]} (1+t^2)\left( rac{G(\zeta)}{t^2} +  rac{ho^2}{2\zeta}ight) \qquad  orall \zeta \in (0,ho]. \end{equation} 
oindent We perform the optimization over $t$ in closed form. Since $G(\zeta) \ge 0$, the function $t \mapsto (1+t^2)\left( rac{G(\zeta)}{t^2} +  rac{ho^2}{2\zeta}ight)$ is convex on $t \in (0,\infty)$ with its minimum at $t^2 =  rac{1}{ho}\sqrt{2\zeta G(\zeta)}$. We will break into two cases depending on whether this minimum occurs before or after the maximum allowable value $\zeta/ho$ for $t$. First consider the $\zeta$ values satisfying  egin{equation}  rac{1}{ho}\sqrt{2\zeta G(\zeta)} \ge  rac{\zeta^2}{ho^2} \end{equation} so that the infimum over $t$ in (ef{eq:t-opt}) is optimized at $t = \zeta/ho$. In this case we require $$\lambda^2 < 2 (1 + \zeta^2/ho^2)\left( rac{G(\zeta) ho^2}{\zeta^2}+ rac{ho^2}{2\zeta}ight) = 2 \left( rac{ho^2 + \zeta^2}{\zeta^2}ight)\left(G(\zeta) +  rac{\zeta}{2}ight),$$ or equivalently, $$G(\zeta) +  rac{\zeta}{2} >  rac{\lambda^2}{2} \cdot rac{\zeta^2}{ho^2+\zeta^2}.$$ If $\zeta <  rac{ho^2}{\lambda^2}$ then this is satisfied because $G(\zeta) \ge 0$, so we can assume $\zeta \ge  rac{ho^2}{\lambda^2}$. We will use the bound  egin{equation} G(\zeta) \ge \zeta \log\left( rac{\zeta(1-ho)}{ep^2}ight). \end{equation} 
oindent The motivation for the bound (ef{eq:G-bound}) comes from the argument in \cite{bmvx} where they compare the hypergeometric random variable to a binomial. One can prove (ef{eq:G-bound}) directly by showing that $G(\zeta) - \zeta \log\left( rac{\zeta(1-ho)}{ep^2}ight)$ is jointly convex in $\zeta,ho$ (since the Hessian has positive trace and determinant) and has a stationary point in the limit $\zeta 	o 0, ho 	o 0$ (at which point the bound holds). Combining (ef{eq:G-bound}) with $ rac{ho^2}{\lambda^2} \le \zeta \le ho$ and the fact that $\zeta \mapsto  rac{\zeta}{ho^2 + \zeta^2}$ is increasing for all $\zeta \le ho$, it is sufficient to show $$\log\left( rac{1-ho}{e\lambda^2}ight) +  rac{1}{2} >  rac{\lambda^2}{4ho}.$$ Rearranging this yields $$ rac{\lambda^2}{4ho} \exp\left( rac{\lambda^2}{4ho}ight) <  rac{1-ho}{4ho\sqrt{e}},$$ or equivalently $ rac{\lambda^2}{4ho} < \mathcal{W}\left( rac{1-ho}{4ho\sqrt{e}}ight)$ where $\mathcal{W}(y)$ (for $y \ge 0$) denotes the (unique) solution to $x e^x = y$. For $y > e$ we have $\mathcal{W}(y) < \log y$ and so for sufficiently small $ho$, we are done provided that $\lambda < 2 \sqrt{-ho \log ho - O(ho)}$ as desired. It remains to consider the other case: the $\zeta$ values for which (ef{eq:zeta-cond}) fails. In this case the infimum over $t$ in (ef{eq:t-opt}) is optimized at $t^2 =  rac{1}{ho} \sqrt{2 \zeta G(\zeta)}$ and (ef{eq:t-opt}) becomes $$ rac{\lambda^2}{2} < \left(\sqrt{G(\zeta)} +  rac{ho}{\sqrt{2\zeta}}ight)^2$$ and so it is sufficient to show $ rac{ho^2}{2\zeta} > -2ho \log ho$. Assume on the contrary that $\zeta \ge  rac{ho}{4\log(1/ho)}$. Combining the bound (ef{eq:G-bound}) with the fact that (ef{eq:zeta-cond}) fails yields $$ rac{\zeta^2}{ho^2} > 2 \log\left( rac{\zeta(1-ho)}{eho^2}ight).$$ Plugging in $\zeta \ge  rac{ho}{4\log(1/ho)}$ gives $$1 \ge  rac{\zeta^2}{ho^2} > 2 \log\left( rac{\zeta(1-ho)}{eho^2}ight) \ge 2 \log\left( rac{(1-ho)}{4eho \log(1/ho)}ight) = 2 \log(1/ho) - 2 \log\log(1/ho) + O(1) = \Theta(\log(1/ho))$$ as $ho 	o 0$, which is a contradiction for sufficiently small $ho$. This completes the proof.

For this case, the argument above is not enough and we will need to use the local subgaussian condition to control the deviations near zero. However, we do not need to use noise conditioning here and will revert to the standard second moment as in prior work (e.g.\ \cite{mrz,bmvx,pwbm}). Starting from the definition (ef{eq:defn-m}) of $m$, we drop the conditioning on $\Omega$ and use the Gaussian moment-generating function to explicitly compute the expectation over $T \sim Q_n$:  egin{align*} m( eta) &\le \Ex_{T \sim Q_n} \exp\left( rac{n\lambda}{2} \langle T, x^{\otimes d} + x'^{\otimes d} angle -  rac{n\lambda^2}{2} ight) \ &= \exp\left( rac{1}{2}\cdot rac{2}{n}\cdot rac{n^2\lambda^2}{4} \langle x^{\otimes d} + x'^{\otimes d}, x^{\otimes d} + x'^{\otimes d} angle -  rac{n\lambda^2}{2} ight) \ &= \exp\left( rac{n\lambda^2}{2} \langle x^{\otimes d}, x'^{\otimes d} angle ight) \ &= \exp\left( rac{n\lambda^2}{2}  eta ight) \defeq m_2( eta). \ \end{align*} 
oindent A helpful trick in performing this computation is to note that $\langle T, x^{\otimes d} angle = \langle T', x^{\otimes d} angle$ where $T'$ is the asymmetric precursor tensor with $\cN(0,2/n)$ entries. Note that $m_2$ is an increasing function of $ eta$. Letting $b = 1$ for $d$ even and $b = 2$ for $d$ odd, we have  egin{align*} M_2 &= \prob{ eta = 0} + b \, \EE_ eta[\one_{ eta \in (0,\eps)} \, m_2( eta)] \ &= \prob{ eta = 0} + b\int_{r=0}^\infty \problr{\one_{ eta \in (0,\eps)} \, m_2( eta) \ge r} \dee r \ &= \prob{ eta = 0} + b\int_{r=0}^\infty \problr{ eta \in (0,\eps) \;	ext{ and }\; \exp\left( rac{n \lambda^2}{2}  eta ight) \ge r} \dee r. \ \intertext{The change of variables $r = \exp\left( rac{n \lambda^2}{2} sight)$ yields} &= \prob{ eta = 0} + b\int_{s=-\infty}^\infty \problr{ eta \in (0,\eps) \;	ext{ and }\;  eta \ge s} \exp\left( rac{n \lambda^2}{2} s ight)  rac{n \lambda^2}{2} \,\dee s \ &\le \prob{ eta = 0} + b\int_{s=-\infty}^0 \prob{ eta > 0} \exp\left( rac{n \lambda^2}{2} s ight)  rac{n \lambda^2}{2} \,\dee s + b\int_{s=0}^{\eps} \problr{ eta \ge s} \exp\left( rac{n \lambda^2}{2} s ight)  rac{n \lambda^2}{2} \,\dee s \ &= 1 +  rac{n \lambda^2 b}{2} \int_{s=0}^{\eps} \prob{\langle x,x' angle \ge s^{1/d}} \exp\left( rac{n \lambda^2}{2} s ight) \dee s \ &\defeq 1 + M_2'. \end{align*} Here we have used the fact $\prob{ eta = 0} + b \prob{ eta > 0} = 1$ (since $\langle x,x' angle$ has a symmetric distribution). Letting $s = t^d$,  egin{align} 
onumber M_2' &\lesssim n \int_{t=0}^{\eps^{1/d}} \prob{\langle x,x' angle \ge t} \exp\left( rac{n\lambda^2}{2} t^dight) t^{d-1} \dee t. \intertext{Let $\eta > 0$, to be chosen later. Choose $\eps$ small enough so that we can apply the local subgaussian bound, obtaining} &\lesssim n \int_{t=0}^{\eps^{1/d}} \exp\left(- rac{n}{2\sigma^2+\eta}t^2 +  rac{n\lambda^2}{2} t^dight) t^{d-1} \dee t. \end{align} First consider the $d \ge 3$ case. Here the $t^2$ term dominates the $t^d$ term, and so (by choosing $\eps$ sufficiently small) we have, for some constant $c > 0$, $$M_2' \lesssim n \int_{t=0}^{\eps^{1/d}} \exp\left(-nc t^2ight) t^{d-1} \dee t \le n \int_{t=0}^\infty \exp\left(-nc t^2ight) t^{d-1} \dee t =  rac{n}{2} (cn)^{-d/2}\, \Gamma(d/2) = o(1).$$ Now consider the $d=2$ case. Now the $t^2$ and $t^d$ terms in (ef{eq:t2td}) are of the same order, so it is important that we have the correct subgaussian constant $\sigma^2$. By hypothesis (v) of the theorem, we are guaranteed that $\lambda < 1/\sigma$ and so by choosing $\eta$ small enough we again have have a constant $c > 0$ such that $$M_2' \lesssim n \int_{t=0}^{\eps^{1/d}} \exp\left(-nc t^2ight) t \,\dee t \le n \int_{t=0}^\infty \exp\left(-nc t^2ight) t \,\dee t =  rac{1}{2c} = O(1).$$ 
oindent In conclusion, we have shown $M_2 = 1 + o(1)$ for $d \ge 3$, and $M_2 = O(1)$ for $d = 2$.
